  "answer": "<p>The best practice for progressive application deployment and smooth rollback in AWS is to implement a blue/green-style setup using separate infrastructure components and DNS routing controls. <b>Launching an additional ELB and new Auto Scaling Group with a new Launch Configuration using your desired AMI, then distributing traffic via Route 53 Weighted Routing</b> minimizes risk, offers granular control, and supports seamless rollback by adjusting weights or setting them to 0% if issues occur.</p><p><i>&quot;Set up a new full stack with fresh instances running the updated version, and switch DNS over to this new stack. If issues arise, simply revert the DNS to the old stack.&quot;</i> (Option D) risks an all-or-nothing cutover, which doesn't offer easy incremental testing and rollback is coarser and slower, as it involves DNS propagation delays.</p><p><i>&quot;Deploy new AMIs containing the application updates, and launch new EC2 instances making up half of your total instance count alongside the current servers.&quot;</i> (Option B) creates mixed environments, complicates ELB health checks, and lacks clear control over which users see which version, especially if not paired with explicit routing or grouping strategies.</p><p><i>&quot;Use AWS Elastic Beanstalk along with application versions. Configure Route 53 to direct a certain percentage of requests to the new environment using Weighted Round Robin records.&quot;</i> (Option C) is plausible but less flexible for low-level configuration than managing ELBs and Auto Scaling Groups directly, and may create vendor lock-in if Beanstalk-specific features are not desired.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Weighted routing policies in Route 53 are ideal for canary and blue/green deployments because they let you dial up/down exposure to the new environment safely and quickly revert traffic if problems emerge.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Suppose your AWS deployment is running behind an Auto Scaling Group with Elastic Load Balancer (ELB). You wish to test a new application version with a portion of live users, while still maintaining the current stable environment. Which approach allows controlled, progressive rollout and safe rollback, using scalable AWS services and minimizing risk?</p>",
